tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Noria and saqiya waterwheels", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "River noria filling an aqueduct, and an ox-driven saqiya over a well.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Hellenistic and Islamic Middle East, saqiya in Ptolemaic Egypt 3rd century BCE; the Hama norias from Byzantine times", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The noria is a river-driven wheel with pots on its rim that empty into an aqueduct at the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "top; the saqiya is an animal-driven chain of pots geared through a right-angle drive.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Hama's norias are up to 20 metres in diameter.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The current pushes paddles on the noria; each pot fills at the bottom and tips at the top", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "into a trough, with no other power source. The saqiya uses a horizontal lantern gear", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "turned by an ox walking in a circle to drive a vertical pot-chain wheel.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:baghdad/bazaar", "color": "aqua"}
